{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Discovered by the Step-7 sibling sweep while fixing bug 747f (blobless snapshot materialization).\n\n747f added `_GIT_TIMEOUT = 300` to `src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`, joining the existing precedent in `src/rebar/_store/push.py:57` and `src/rebar/_store/sync.py:58` (`_GIT_TIMEOUT = 30`). The sweep found the *timeout* half of that bug class still live at other network-capable git call sites, each of which can block indefinitely on a stuck remote or a hung credential helper. 747f proved this is not theoretical: an unbounded git call on the materialize path produced 40 sequential per-object fetch RPCs and a measured ~2.1-hour hang.\n\n## SCOPE — deliberately narrowed by the operator; recorded here, not silently\n\nThe original sweep listed 8 call sites. This ticket now covers SIX of them, in three modules. The other two were SPLIT OUT, not dropped:\n\n**DEFERRED to task 983f-ee85-1e06-428e (rainbow-interested-kusimanse), linked `discovered_from` this ticket:**\n- `src/rebar/_commands/init.py` — `fetch <remote> <branch>`\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` — `push origin HEAD:tickets`\n\n**Reason for the split:** bug 01e8-302b-ab8f-4220 is IN PROGRESS in another session covering store/init/sync divergence and has already modified `init.py` and the fsck path. 01e8 declares no `file_impact`, so the overlap cannot be verified precisely, and the operator chose not to guess rather than risk a conflicting edit. 983f carries a `depends_on` link to 01e8 and is blocked until it closes.\n\n## The six in-scope sites, re-verified against origin/main @ ed9d5e28d\n\n**A. `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock.py` (2 sites) — ALREADY BOUNDED; the original sweep note is STALE.**\n- `_remote_ref_absent` -> `ls-remote --exit-code` passes `timeout=_REMOTE_TIMEOUT_SECS` (30.0s).\n- `_push_lease_cas` -> `push --force-with-lease` passes `timeout=_REMOTE_TIMEOUT_SECS` (30.0s).\n\nThis is structurally guaranteed, not incidental: the module's single git seam `_git(repo_root, args, *, timeout: float, ...)` declares `timeout` as a REQUIRED keyword-only parameter with no default, and it holds the module's only `run_git` call. An unbounded git call is therefore unrepresentable in that module. A timeout already surfaces as `RefLockTimeoutError` naming the operation and the bound, and is fail-closed (treated as HELD). **No edit is required or made here** — which also honours the standing instruction not to touch `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/**` while it is owned by a parallel session.\n\n**B. `src/rebar/review_bot/gerrit_client.py::clone_change_ref` — UNBOUNDED, fixed by this ticket.**\nFive `subprocess.run` calls, none passing a timeout: `git init`, `fetch --depth 2 <repo_url> <revision_ref>`, `checkout FETCH_HEAD`, `remote add origin`, `fetch --depth 1 origin refs/heads/tickets`. The two fetches are network-capable. Additionally `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` is neither an `OSError` nor a `CalledProcessError`, so it was outside the existing `except` tuple — a timeout would have escaped the token-redacting handler entirely, and `TimeoutExpired.cmd` carries the `user:token@host` fetch URL, so an unredacted timeout is also a credential-leak path.\n\n**C. `src/rebar/opcert_service/workspace.py` — UNBOUNDED, fixed by this ticket.**\nThe single `_git(cwd, *args)` helper calls `subprocess.run(...)` with no timeout, so EVERY git call in the module is unbounded, including the two network-capable ones: `fetch --quiet review <branch>` and `fetch --quiet tickets <branch>`. A `TimeoutExpired` would also bypass `_git_ok`'s `WorkspaceError` conversion.\n\n## Value chosen: `_GIT_TIMEOUT = 300`, matching 747f rather than `_store`\n\nThe two live modules both do a COLD fetch into a freshly created directory (`gerrit_client` `git init`s a new dest per change; `workspace` fetches into a fresh `mkdtemp`), and `workspace`'s fetches are not even shallow. That is 747f's profile — \"a legitimately large RPC, legitimately minutes on a cold clone\" — not the `_store` profile, which is an INCREMENTAL ref-sized push/fetch against an already-warm clone and so is correctly bound at 30s. Choosing 30s here would risk breaking a working cold-clone path; 300s converts an unbounded hang into a bounded, diagnosable failure without narrowing any legitimate one. One constant per module (not a per-call-site matrix) matches the existing `_GIT_TIMEOUT` precedent instead of inventing a new mechanism.\n\n## Resolved: the `git archive` question (was flagged UNJUDGED)\n\n`src/rebar/llm/workflow/snapshot.py` materializes via `git archive --format=tar <sha>`, and the sweep asked whether it degrades like the `read-tree`/`checkout-index` pair 747f fixed. **Measured read-only, answer NO.** On a 40-file `--filter=blob:none` fixture with `uploadpack.allowFilter` enabled (git 2.55.0), GIT_TRACE2_EVENT `child_start` counting shows 40 missing blobs resolved by exactly ONE batched child `git fetch ... --filter=blob:none --stdin` — versus 40 separate sequential fetches for the primitive pair 747f fixed. `git archive` therefore DOES batch its promisor prefetch and is NOT an instance of the 747f bug class. No change made, no follow-up ticket filed; the file is owned by a parallel session and was inspected read-only only.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (amended to the six-site scope)\n\n1. Of the six in-scope sites: the two `_ref_lock.py` sites are verified already bounded (documented above, no edit); the two `gerrit_client.py` fetches and the two `workspace.py` fetches pass an explicit timeout.\n2. A timeout surfaces as an ACTIONABLE error naming the git operation that timed out and the bound it exceeded — never a bare `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, never a hang. In `gerrit_client` it must also be routed through the existing token-redaction path.\n3. A held-out RED->GREEN test proves both modules pass a timeout AND convert a timing-out call into the module's descriptive error type.\n4. `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default suite exit zero.\n5. The two deferred `_commands/` sites are recorded on task 983f-ee85-1e06-428e with their blocking reason — NOT silently dropped from scope."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785531862226624001,"uuid":"4c501a6b-5ce8-4365-ba86-555761d36c39"}